WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.673
Containers should run with the least privilege needed for the application to operate.

2
00:00:05.673 --> 00:00:17.718
Running as root inside the container can make mistakes more consequential, especially when writable paths, mounted volumes, broad capabilities, or host integrations are involved.

3
00:00:17.718 --> 00:00:21.370
A non-root runtime user is a practical default for many .

4
00:00:21.370 --> 00:00:25.955
NET services. Moving to non-root is not only a single Dockerfile line.

5
00:00:25.955 --> 00:00:35.824
File ownership, working directories, temporary directories, upload locations, log behavior, certificate access, and bind mounts all need review.

6
00:00:35.824 --> 00:00:44.295
The application should know where it is allowed to write, and those locations should be intentionally created and permissioned.

7
00:00:44.295 --> 00:00:50.589
Privileged containers and broad Linux capabilities should be rare, documented, and reviewed.

8
00:00:50.589 --> 00:00:58.904
Many . NET web applications do not need extra capabilities, host namespace access, device mounts, or broad filesystem access.

9
00:00:58.904 --> 00:01:05.509
Reducing runtime privilege helps make production behavior more predictable and easier to operate.

10
00:01:05.509 --> 00:01:12.892
Read-only root filesystems can reduce accidental writes and make unexpected filesystem changes more visible.

11
00:01:12.892 --> 00:01:23.460
They also require planning. An application may still need writable locations for temporary files, uploads, caches, logs, diagnostics, or framework features.

12
00:01:23.460 --> 00:01:28.434
Those paths should be explicit and separate from the application binaries.

13
00:01:28.434 --> 00:01:40.557
Capabilities, seccomp, AppArmor, SELinux, user namespaces, and runtime security options may be owned by a platform team, but developers should understand their application impact.

14
00:01:40.557 --> 00:01:53.690
Application code should not assume it can install tools at runtime, write anywhere in the container, change filesystem state casually, or depend on permissions that production policy does not grant.
